item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"In stable SCOPe 2.08, the alcohol-dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains (family c.2.1.1, SUNID 51736), CoA-binding domains (c.2.1.8, SUNID 51900), and potassium-channel NAD-binding domains (c.2.1.9, SUNID 63944) are distinct families within the same NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold superfamily c.2.1 (SUNID 51735). All also lie in fold c.2 (SUNID 51734) and class c, whose retrieved fold annotation describes a three-layer α/β/α core with a six-stranded parallel β-sheet in order 321456. The shared superfamily—not merely the shared fold—means SCOPe supports probable common ancestry of their classified domains; their separation into three families represents substantial specialization and does not support a closer, same-family relationship.

Function must not be transferred across these families from their names alone. In particular, alcohol-dehydrogenase reaction chemistry or substrate specificity cannot be assigned to the CoA-binding or channel-associated domains; CoA binding cannot be assigned to the other two; and NAD binding, ion-channel regulation, or whole potassium-channel function cannot be assigned outside c.2.1.9. The c.2.1.1 annotation additionally says that an N-terminal all-beta domain defines that family, so its C-terminal Rossmann domain alone does not establish the complete alcohol-dehydrogenase architecture or function. The c.2.1.9 annotation records an automatic mapping to Pfam PF02254; this is provenance, not biochemical validation or evidence of low quality.

The shared superfamily can justify considering remote structural templates for the conserved Rossmann core, but not treating family members as interchangeable templates for ligand-binding residues, loops, insertions, oligomerization, catalytic sites, or regulatory interfaces. Stronger functional transfer requires target-specific sequence and core-position correspondence, active-site or ligand-contact conservation in equivalent structural contexts, ligand-bound structural evidence, compatible domain architecture, and biochemical binding or catalytic assays; detailed evolutionary claims may also require phylogenetic and literature evidence. SCOPe hierarchy alone also cannot select a best representative: quality records for every candidate and the relevant ligand, conformational, and biological states would be required.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info: /api/v1/; /api/v1/releases/current","mcp__scope__search_scop (identifier discovery; final claims verified with hierarchy endpoints)","mcp__scope__list_scop_children: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/children?limit=100","mcp__scope__get_scop_node: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/homology"]}]}
